"Score each option 1-5 on each criterion. There is no NA: a field that says \"none\" or \"none known\" scores 1. Give each score a reason of at most 20 words. Score from the quantities, sources and gaps the option names; ignore verdict words such as COMPUTE-BOUND or NOT COMPUTE-BOUND. Also label each option SAME, ADJACENT or ELSEWHERE against the root question, with a reason: SAME if it asks the root question or a sub-question of it, ADJACENT if it has the root's subject but asks a different question, ELSEWHERE if its subject is different. Decide the subject from the title and summary; count root_link only if the summary itself describes the root question's object of study. Do not add up the scores. Decide only from the text below; do not use tools.
Root question: Do more members of a machine-learned weather ensemble buy better forecasts of rare extremes, or does error shared by all members cap the skill?
Criteria:
stakes: Suppose the question this option raises were answered, either way. 5 = a choice someone faces (what to buy, build, collect or scale next) turns on the answer, and either answer would move it; 3 = the answer would refine a belief but no choice turns on it, or one answer is already much more likely than the other; 1 = either answer is already expected, or nothing would change. Do not score how famous or important the field is, or how close it is to the root question.
open: Has published work already measured how the answer changes with the quantity compute buys here (samples, ensemble members, resolution, model runs, model size, search width), across the range that $1,000 of Lambda on-demand GPU time (for scale, about 250 H100-hours) can reach? 5 = names the quantity and where published measurements stop (or that none exist), and the unmeasured range starts within that reach; 3 = the unmeasured range is reachable only through a smaller stand-in system, or published work already covers part of the reachable range; 1 = names no compute-bought quantity, or published work already covers everything within that reach, so the open range lies only beyond it.
reshape: Does the option name a part of the work that one answer waits on step after step, and a reason those steps might not have to run in order? 5 = names that chain and roughly how long it is; one answer leaves most of a GPU idle (a small state, or few runs needed, so copies side by side would not fill it); names what would have to be true of the system for the steps to be computed at the same time, in a form a cheap measurement could check; and no cited source already computes this chain in parallel for this system; 3 = names the chain and a way to restructure it, but one of these fails: the answer needs many independent runs, so copies side by side already fill the hardware; one run already fills a GPU; or no checkable condition is named; 1 = sequential says none, or the only parallelism is independent pieces (copies, a sweep, one job per GPU), or a cited source already computes this chain in parallel for this system. A method published for a different system does not lower this score. Do not score how famous the method or field is.
Options, one per line:
{\"id\":\"O\",\"title\":\"Ensemble size for rare extremes in a machine-learned weather ensemble\",\"summary\":\"ECMWF runs its machine-learned ensemble, AIFS-ENS, operationally with 50 members, and the model weights are public. An extra member needs one GPU run rather than a supercomputer slot, so ensembles of hundreds or thousands look affordable. Study whether 10 to 1,000 members improve probability forecasts of rare extremes, such as top-percentile wind, temperature or rainfall. Score against ERA5 reanalysis and station observations, which the model did not produce. The open question is whether more members keep helping or whether error shared by every member caps the skill.\",\"root_link\":\"SAME: the director's C1 question made concrete on one open, operational model.\",\"compute_open\":\"Ensemble members per forecast, from about 10 to 1,000. Unknown: whether skill on top-percentile events keeps rising past the operational 50 members, or flattens because members share model error. The closest prior, HENS, ran 7,424 members of a different model (SFNO) for summer 2023.\",\"sequential\":\"not given\"}
{\"id\":\"V1\",\"title\":\"Ensemble size for rare extremes in the HENS SFNO large ensemble\",\"summary\":\"HENS ran 7,424 SFNO members per day for 92 summer-2023 dates and showed that huge ensembles sample 4-sigma tails and cut outliers, but it did not score top-percentile probabilities against ERA5. Run 1,000-member SFNO ensembles on a subset of those HENS dates, subsample 10-1,000 members, and score top-percentile wind, temperature and rainfall probabilities against ERA5 with fair CRPS and Brier scores. The open question is whether HENS's tail-sampling result turns into extreme-probability skill past the operational 50 members, or saturates because SFNO's members share error. If skill saturates, report the member count where it does.\",\"root_link\":\"SAME: the director's C1 question made concrete on the model HENS used (SFNO), which the original idea cites only as prior context.\",\"compute_open\":\"Ensemble members per forecast, about 10 to 1,000, on HENS's summer-2023 dates. Unknown: whether SFNO's extreme-probability skill saturates at the same member count as AIFS-ENS or keeps rising toward HENS's 7,424.\",\"sequential\":\"not given\"}
{\"id\":\"V2\",\"title\":\"Ensemble size for rare extremes across a multi-season AIFS-ENS sample\",\"summary\":\"The original runs 7 dates x 1,000 members, so its rare-event scores rest on seven synoptic situations. Spend the same total, 7,500 member-runs, on 30 dates spread across seasons with 250 members each, then subsample 10-250 members and score top-percentile wind, temperature and rainfall probabilities against ERA5 and station observations with fair CRPS and Brier scores. The open question becomes whether the member-count conclusion holds across seasons and weather regimes, or whether the seven-date answer is regime-specific. Report the saturation member count per season.\",\"root_link\":\"SAME: the director's C1 question, made concrete on a multi-season sample rather than seven dates.\",\"compute_open\":\"Forecast dates and seasons, about 30, at 250 members. Unknown: whether the saturation member count is stable across seasons or regime-dependent.\",\"sequential\":\"not given\"}
{\"id\":\"V3\",\"title\":\"AIFS-ENS ensemble size judged against the operational physical ensemble\",\"summary\":\"Score AIFS-ENS not only against ERA5 reanalysis but against ECMWF's operational physical ensemble, IFS ENS, on the same dates, treating IFS ENS as the benchmark its 50 members must match. Run 1,000 AIFS-ENS v1 members for 7 dates, subsample 10-1,000, and compare top-percentile wind, temperature and rainfall probabilities from both ensembles with fair CRPS and Brier scores. The open question is whether adding AIFS-ENS members past 50 closes the gap to the physical ensemble's extreme probabilities, or whether shared ML model error leaves a floor IFS ENS does not have. Report the member count at which the gap stops shrinking.\",\"root_link\":\"SAME: the director's C1 question, made concrete against the operational physical ensemble as the reference forecast.\",\"compute_open\":\"Ensemble members per forecast, 10-1,000, plus paired IFS ENS members on the same dates. Unknown: whether the AIFS-ENS-to-IFS-ENS extreme-probability gap shrinks with more members or plateaus from shared ML error.\",\"sequential\":\"not given\"}

Return only one JSON value, with no other text, that matches this JSON Schema:
{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"id\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"distance\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"enum\":[\"SAME\",\"ADJACENT\",\"ELSEWHERE\"]},\"distance_reason\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"scores\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"criterion\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"value\":{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":1,\"maximum\":5},\"reason\":{\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"criterion\",\"value\",\"reason\"]}}},\"required\":[\"id\",\"distance\",\"distance_reason\",\"scores\"]}}},\"required\":[\"items\"]}"

